<span class="kicker">2026-07-25 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How Much Does a Paver Walkway Cost?</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>A paver walkway typically costs between 8 and 35 dollars per square foot, depending on whether you build it yourself or hire a professional, along with the specific site conditions and design choices involved. That’s a wide range, and the reasons behind it come down to a handful of factors that either add or remove cost from the final number, most of which have nothing to do with the price of the pavers themselves.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, we quote paver walkway projects across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, on a regular basis, and the same questions come up almost every time. This guide breaks down exactly what drives that cost up or down, where homeowners typically get surprised, and how to figure out a realistic number for your specific yard before you get a quote or start buying materials.</p>
<h2><b>Paver Walkway Cost at a Glance</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Approach</b></td><td><b>Cost Per Square Foot</b></td><td><b>What’s Included</b></td></tr><tr><td>DIY</td><td>8 to 15 dollars</td><td>Materials only, tool rental extra</td></tr><tr><td>Professionally installed</td><td>18 to 35 dollars</td><td>Materials, labor, equipment, base work, warranty</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The DIY range covers materials only, pavers, base gravel, sand, and edge restraints, plus whatever tools you need to rent. The professional range includes labor, equipment, proper base preparation, and typically a workmanship warranty, which is why it sits meaningfully higher per square foot. Neither number includes design work, permits if required, or removal of an existing surface, which we’ll get into further down.</p>
<h2><b>Sample Cost for a Typical Walkway</b></h2>
<p>To put these numbers in context, here’s roughly what a few common walkway sizes might cost at each end of the range.</p>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Walkway Size</b></td><td><b>DIY Cost Estimate</b></td><td><b>Professional Cost Estimate</b></td></tr><tr><td>50 square feet</td><td>400 to 750 dollars</td><td>900 to 1,750 dollars</td></tr><tr><td>75 square feet</td><td>600 to 1,125 dollars</td><td>1,350 to 2,625 dollars</td></tr><tr><td>100 square feet</td><td>800 to 1,500 dollars</td><td>1,800 to 3,500 dollars</td></tr><tr><td>150 square feet</td><td>1,200 to 2,250 dollars</td><td>2,700 to 5,250 dollars</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These are rough estimates, and actual pricing will shift depending on the factors below. A short, straight walkway with simple grading tends to land at the lower end. Anything involving curves, slopes, or a longer overall length pushes toward the higher end, sometimes past it if the site has unusual challenges.</p>
<h2><b>What Actually Drives the Cost of a Paver Walkway</b></h2>
<h3><b>Length and Width</b></h3>
<p>The most obvious factor is simply how much square footage you’re covering. A wider walkway or a longer path directly increases both material quantity and labor time, and cost scales close to linearly with square footage once other factors are held constant. Width matters more than people expect here too, a walkway that jumps from 3 feet to 4 feet wide adds roughly 33 percent more material for the same length, which is easy to underestimate when sketching out a plan.</p>
<h3><b>Site Conditions and Prep Work</b></h3>
<p>Flat, easy to access yards cost less to work with than sites with slopes, poor drainage, or heavy clay soil that needs extra excavation. If tree roots, existing hardscape, or drainage issues are present, expect additional cost for removal, regrading, or extra base material. Access matters too, a walkway in a backyard reachable only through a narrow side gate often costs more than the same size project in a front yard, simply because materials and equipment have to be moved by hand rather than wheeled or driven in directly.</p>
<h3><b>Paver Pattern Complexity</b></h3>
<p>Simple patterns like running bond or stack bond install faster, which keeps labor cost down. More intricate patterns, like herringbone or basketweave, require more cutting and precise alignment, which adds labor time even when the paver material itself is the same price. Our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-lay-a-paver-walkway/"> how to lay a paver walkway</a> walks through how these patterns differ in difficulty and finished look, which is worth reviewing before you commit to a design that might add unexpected labor cost.</p>
<h3><b>Edge Shape and Borders</b></h3>
<p>A straight walkway with clean edges costs less to install than one with curves, tapered sections, or a decorative border using a different paver. Curved cuts take more time and produce more waste material, both of which add to the total. A contrasting border paver, while a nice visual touch, effectively means ordering and managing two separate paver types, which adds a bit of coordination cost even on a small project.</p>
<h3><b>Base Depth and Preparation</b></h3>
<p>Proper base depth matters for long-term durability, and it’s not an area worth cutting corners on to save money upfront. A thinner or poorly compacted base might lower initial cost, but it significantly increases the odds of settling, shifting, or cracking down the road, which usually costs more to fix than it would have to do right the first time. For more detail on what proper base prep involves, see our<a href="/blog/paver-base-depth-for-patios-proper-installation-tips/"> guide to paver base depth</a>.</p>
<h3><b>DIY Versus Professional Installation</b></h3>
<p>Doing the work yourself removes labor cost from the equation, but it adds tool rental, your own time, and some risk if base prep or leveling isn’t done correctly. Hiring a professional costs more upfront but typically comes with a workmanship warranty and a result that’s less likely to need repair within the first few years.</p>
<h3><b>Removal of an Existing Surface</b></h3>
<p>If your walkway is replacing an existing concrete path, gravel trail, or worn-out paver surface, factor in demolition and disposal cost separately from the new installation. Breaking up and hauling away old concrete in particular adds a meaningful line item that’s easy to forget when comparing quotes, since it doesn’t show up in a simple per square foot number for the new pavers.</p>

